tions 7 million  for small business growth and the  remaining 5.5 million to major  infrastructure projects a yeren to their  investment JP Morgan earmarked 3.5  million dollars specifically for loans  to minority-owned businesses in a city  where the majority of the population is  black and have had a hard time accessing  capital even during periods where  interest rates were at an all-time low  after the recession banks got the  bailout but then they wouldn't load  anybody any money unless you had a  stellar credit rating or a big cash cow  behind so you just put it out of your  mind  don't even go at this couple angel and  Martin got to $100,000 lines of credit  from one of JP Morgan's local partners  Detroit Development Fund and yeah their  electrical contracting businesses doing  a lot better now for JP Morgan a  low-interest loan of this nature is  categorized as an act of corporate  responsibility but they're very clear  that this isn't charity work they aren't  giving away the money and we are making  an investment in Detroit so it begs the  question why is a bank with over two  trillion dollars in assets making the  focus of their philanthropy work trying  to revitalize an entire city from  we asked JP Morgan's head of corporate  responsibility who is touring a skilled  manufacturing training city Detroit is  not growing and we're a big bank in  Detroit that's not a good thing for us  from a business proposition right in  other words if there's no money flowing  there's no banking to be done the Motor  City has basically been in decline for  decades the median household income is  just $25,000 2/5 of the city population  lives in poverty and the City Public  Schools are some of the worst performing  nationwide everyone seems to agree that  Detroit has a lot of problems and it's  gonna take a lot of money to fix these  problems but who should be responsible  for ponying up that money is a different  question  the expectation for a long time has been  that go